## Problem

Draft models are typically trained to match the target’s full distribution at a given context, whereas speculative-decoding acceptance is determined by the probabilities assigned to individual proposed tokens. Because these proposals usually come from a small set of plausible candidates, acceptance is particularly sensitive to the relative probability margins within that set. Neither forward nor reverse KL directly supervises the relative spacing among these candidates. Forward KL encourages broad coverage, whereas reverse KL is more mode-seeking; in both cases, the margins that influence acceptance emerge only indirectly from the overall distribution-matching objective.

## Novelty: Supervising How the Output Shifts

We introduce a logit-shift loss that supervises not only the draft’s output at the ground-truth context, but also how that output changes when a single token in the context is replaced. This objective directly constrains how the draft re-ranks plausible continuations after its context changes—a behavior that standard single-context distillation does not explicitly supervise. The resulting draft produces longer accepted prefixes and higher throughput with no additional inference cost.

## Motivation

Full-distribution matching does not directly optimize the candidate-level margins that govern acceptance. More importantly, matching these margins at a ground-truth context does not ensure that they change correctly when the preceding token changes. Replacing that token with another plausible candidate may leave the likely continuations largely unchanged while shifting their relative probabilities. Standard distillation supervises the output at each ground-truth context, but not how the output responds to such a replacement. We therefore train the draft to match the target’s logit shift between the original and substituted contexts, while retaining the standard distillation objective to supervise the distributions themselves.


## Method

**Substituted context.** At position (t), let (a_t) denote the draft’s top-1 prediction conditioned on the prefix (x_{<t}):

[
a_t = \arg\max_v z^D_v(x_{<t}).
]

We apply a substitution when

[
a_t \neq \arg\max_v z^T_v(x_{<t})
\quad\text{and}\quad
a_t \in \operatorname{top}\text{-}k!\left(z^T(x_{<t})\right),
\qquad k=3.
]

The resulting substituted context is

[
x^{\mathrm{sub}}_{\le t}
========================

(x_1,\ldots,x_{t-1},a_t),
]

whereas the original context is

[
x_{\le t}
=========

(x_1,\ldots,x_{t-1},x_t).
]

The first condition ensures that the draft’s proposal differs from the target’s preferred token. The second restricts the substitution to a token that the target also considers plausible, keeping the two contexts close and focusing the objective on alternatives that the draft might actually propose.

**Logit-shift loss.** Both models are evaluated on the original and substituted contexts. Define centered logits as

[
\operatorname{center}(z)
========================

z-\frac{1}{|V|}\sum_{v\in V}z_v.
]

The target and draft shifts are

[
\Delta_T
========

## \operatorname{center}!\left(z^T(x^{\mathrm{sub}}_{\le t})\right)

\operatorname{center}!\left(z^T(x_{\le t})\right),
]

[
\Delta_D
========

## \operatorname{center}!\left(z^D(x^{\mathrm{sub}}_{\le t})\right)

\operatorname{center}!\left(z^D(x_{\le t})\right).
]

We minimize

[
\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{shift}}
============================

\left\lVert \Delta_D-\Delta_T \right\rVert_2^2,
]

and combine it with the standard distillation objective:

[
\mathcal{L}
===========

\mathcal{L}*{\mathrm{reg}}
+
\lambda \mathcal{L}*{\mathrm{shift}},
\qquad
\lambda=0.1,
]

where (\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{reg}}) is left unchanged.

**Why center the logits?** For any constant (c), the logits (z) and (z+c\mathbf{1}) define the same distribution under softmax. This additive component therefore contains no probability-relevant information for the draft to match. Centering removes it and retains only changes that can affect the output distribution.

**Why supervise the shift rather than the substituted output?** The original and substituted contexts differ by only one plausible token, so their output logits contain substantial shared structure. Directly distilling the full output at the substituted context would therefore largely repeat supervision already provided by (\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{reg}}). Taking the difference cancels this shared component and isolates the change caused by the substitution.

